[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0001675
name: European American and Polish height panels
def: "Two case-control panels of self-described white adults sampled from the tails of the height distribution (the 5th to 10th and the 90th to 95th percentiles), one of European Americans born in the United States and one of people born in Poland, studied under the approval of the Institutional Review Board of Children's Hospital, Boston, and genotyped to validate variants associated with adult height." [] {dcterms:contributor="Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09", IAO:0000119="PMID:17767157", IAO:0000119="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnature09410/MediaObjects/41586_2010_BFnature09410_MOESM23_ESM.pdf"}
synonym: "EAPEH" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "European American and Poland Extreme Height panels" RELATED []
instance_of: coho:COHO_0000000 ! cohort
property_value: coho:numberOfParticipants "3207" xsd:integer {IAO:0000116="combined extreme-height adult panels genotyped in the 2007 HMGA2 study", xref="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3086278/", comment="We genotyped an additional 29,098 individuals of European ancestry from five studies, including three population-based studies (15,167 adults and 6,827 children), a type 2 diabetes case-control set (3,897 adults) and a collection of 3,207 adults sampled from the near-extremes of the height distribution (the 5th to 10th and 90th to 95th percentiles; Supplementary Table 1)."}
property_value: IAO:0000112 "PMID:20881960" xsd:string {dcterms:title="Hundreds of variants clustered in genomic loci and biological pathways affect human height.", xref="PMID:20881960", xref="https://media.springernature.com/original/springer-static/esm/art%3A10.1038%2Fnature09410/MediaObjects/41586_2010_BFnature09410_MOESM23_ESM.pdf", comment="Association results for 33 SNPs selected randomly among the 180 SNPs that reached genome-wide significance (P=5x10-8 ) in the Stage 1 meta-analysis and genotyped in European American (N=2,181) and Poland (N=1,009) panels at tails of height distribution."}

[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0001676
name: obsolete EAS
comment: Obsoleted 2026-09-24. EAS, the PGS Catalog cohort id used for the type 2 diabetes score PGS000020 (Läll et al. 2016), is the DIAGRAM consortium’s EAS, which its 2012 meta-analysis (PMID:22885922) describes as funded by the British Heart Foundation (RG/98002) and genotyped in Edinburgh, the funding of the Edinburgh Artery Study (PMC5841831: EAS (Edinburgh Artery Study). EAS is funded by the British Heart Foundation (Programme Grant RG/98002)); the Catalog also lists the study under its own name as EAS-Edinburgh. Replaced by COHO:0001677, Edinburgh Artery Study, which carries the acronym EAS. Found by the review of 2026-09-24.
is_obsolete: true
replaced_by: coho:COHO_0001677

[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0001677
name: Edinburgh Artery Study
def: "A study of peripheral arterial disease in the general population of Edinburgh, Scotland, based on an age-stratified sample of 1,592 men and women aged 55 to 74 selected from the age-sex registers of ten general practices in the city and assessed by questionnaire and ankle brachial pressure index, funded by the British Heart Foundation and genotyped for genetic studies such as the DIAGRAM consortium’s meta-analysis of type 2 diabetes and the UCLEB consortium’s Mendelian randomisation analyses." [] {IAO:0000119="PMID:22885922", IAO:0000119="PMID:25979724", dcterms:contributor="Claude Fable 5.1 (Anthropic), 2026-09", IAO:0000119="PMID:1917239"}
subset: coho1:GWAS_subset
subset: coho1:PGS_subset
