[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0002003
name: obsolete Limburg Cancer Registry
comment: Obsoleted 2026-09-27. LIKAR is the Limburg Cancer Registry of Belgium, which is not a study; its only genetic use is as the source of the cases of the Belgian case-control study on bladder cancer risk (PMID:21750109), so the id goes on that study and the registry's name is not carried over. Replaced by COHO:0001547 (Belgian case-control study on bladder cancer risk), which now carries this term's names. Found by a check of the placeholders against the papers the PGS Catalog links to them or found by their names, 2026-09-25.
is_obsolete: true
replaced_by: coho:COHO_0001547

[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0002004
name: Linz Peripheral Arterial Disease Study
def: "A prospectively designed hospital-based case-control and follow-up study of lower extremity peripheral arterial disease in Linz, Austria, which enrolled consecutively, from April 2000 to April 2002, all patients with symptomatic chronic atherosclerotic peripheral arterial disease admitted to the Konventhospital Barmherzige Brueder Linz, and from 2001 to 2004 controls without atherosclerotic disease admitted to the same hospital for minor health problems, matched by age, sex and diabetes status, to evaluate biomarkers and genetic variants for peripheral arterial disease and the long-term outcome of the patients through the Austrian Mortality Registry." [] {IAO:0000119="PMID:18325332", dcterms:contributor="Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09", IAO:0000119="https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5054299/fullTextXML", IAO:0000119="https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5692147/fullTextXML"}
synonym: "Linz Peripheral Arterial Disease (LIPAD) Study" RELATED []
synonym: "Linz Peripheral Arterial Disease study" RELATED []
synonym: "LIPAD" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
instance_of: coho:COHO_0000000 ! cohort
relationship: coho:has_data_collection_location http://dbpedia.org/resource/Austria {xref="https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC5054299/fullTextXML", comment="From April 2000 to April 2002 we consecutively enrolled all patients with symptomatic chronic atherosclerotic PAD admitted to a single tertiary care hospital in Austria (Konventhospital Barmherzige Brueder, Linz).3–5 PAD was diagnosed by the vascular surgeon attending during index hospitalization."}
relationship: coho:has_data_collection_location http://dbpedia.org/resource/Europe
