[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0002296
name: obsolete Program in Sleep at Emory University
comment: Obsoleted 2026-09-24. Checked 2026-09-23: The PGS Catalog id PSEU is not attached to any score or sample set in the Catalog's current data (REST /cohort/PSEU returns nothing; absent from cohort_papers.json), so the Catalog paper that used it could not be read; the identification of the sample rests on the name, which matches the restless legs syndrome sample of Emory University's Department of Neurology and Program in Sleep. The Program in Sleep at Emory University is a clinical sleep programme in Atlanta (United States), not a study. The only sample of that name found is the restless legs syndrome case-control collection of the Department of Neurology and Program in Sleep, which the RLS GWAS PMID:33239738 calls just 'Emory cohort' / 'US Emory' (1417 subjects, 696 RLS cases): a clinic's sample with no study name of its own, like the other place-named samples judged not to be cohorts (e.g. KULEUVEN, Toronto). The Catalog id presumably denotes that collection in an RLS polygenic score paper that could not be identified. Found by a check of the placeholder names flagged as institutions or places against their sources, 2026-09-24.
is_obsolete: true

[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0002297
name: obsolete Perlegen Sciences Genotyped Discovery Samples
comment: Obsoleted 2026-09-27. The Perlegen Sciences genotyped discovery samples are the discovery sample of the psoriasis GWAS of the Collaborative Association Study of Psoriasis ('Perlegen Sciences (Mountain View, CA, USA) genotyped discovery samples', PMID:19169254). The Catalog's name describes that sample and is not carried over. Replaced by COHO:0001152 (Collaborative Association Study of Psoriasis), which now carries this term's names. Found by a search for the cohorts behind placeholder names with no known paper, 2026-09-27.
is_obsolete: true
replaced_by: coho:COHO_0001152

[Instance]
id: coho:COHO_0002298
name: Pumwani Sex Workers Cohort
def: "An open cohort of female sex workers in Pumwani, Nairobi, Kenya, established in 1985 with follow-up visits twice a year and studied with the University of Nairobi and the University of Manitoba, in which a subgroup of women remain uninfected by HIV-1 despite heavy exposure through sex work; it is used to study natural resistance to HIV-1 infection, seroconversion and disease progression, including host genetic studies of HLA and other variants." [] {dcterms:contributor="Claude Opus 5.5 (Anthropic), 2026-09", IAO:0000119="PMID:29295533", IAO:0000119="PMID:9840285", IAO:0000119="PMID:22915813", IAO:0000119="PMID:24992306"}
